Title: A reviewer panel that runs zero seats reports success
Created: 2026-09-03
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-09-03T13:06Z Minh Ha-Duong created
2026-09-06T17:10Z claude note Renumbered from 0853, which MR #790 had filed for another ticket; jumped clear of the 0854 frontier

--- body ---

## Context

Reviewing a contribution prepared for a third-party project on 2026-09-03, the
panel was asked for on the fork's own merge request and reported:

```
request: WARN forge-bot seat 'copilot' request failed (fail-open)
request: WARN seat 'openrouter-frontier' failed (fail-open; ...)
request: WARN seat 'openrouter-budget' failed (fail-open; ...)
request: 0 cli/model seat(s) ran for MR #10
```

and exited **0**. Every seat had failed, no finding existed anywhere, and the
command said so only on stderr, in a line a caller does not read. A caller that
gates on the exit status — which is what an exit status is for — cannot tell that
run apart from one where three seats reviewed the diff and were content.

That is the shape `coding-bash.md` already names: a check whose all-clear is
indistinguishable from its "I could not look" is not a check. It is worth more
here than in most places, because the panel exists precisely to be the
independent eye, and a silent absence of review is the failure it cannot afford.

Per-seat fail-open is correct and stays (0205 rule 2): one seat must never block
a verdict. What is wrong is the aggregate. No seat running is not a lenient
verdict, it is no verdict.

## The second half, which is a documentation defect and not a code one

The same run first failed with `pathspec 'pooling-per-model' did not match any
file(s)`, because `REPO_ROOT` defaults to the harness checkout and the branch
under review lived in another repository. The lever for that already exists —
`REVIEWERS_REPO`, and `REVIEWERS_PR_BRANCH` for the branch — and neither appears
in `usage_text`, so the operator's visible options are a merge-request number and
a branch, both of which resolve against the wrong tree. The fix is to document
them where the operator looks, not to add an argument that is already there.

Noted because the first draft of this ticket proposed adding a `--repo` option
that duplicates `REVIEWERS_REPO`. Reading the script settled it; grepping for the
lever before proposing one is the standing rule.

## Relevant files

- `skills/reviewers/reviewers.sh` — `request`, the `ran` counter, `usage_text`
- `tests/test_reviewers.sh` — where the case belongs

## Actions

1. Count cli/model seats attempted beside the ones that ran.
2. When at least one was attempted and none ran, say so on stderr and exit
   non-zero. Keep per-seat fail-open exactly as it is.
3. A roster of forge-bot seats alone still exits 0: nothing local was attempted,
   so there is nothing to report as absent.
4. Document `REVIEWERS_REPO` and `REVIEWERS_PR_BRANCH` in `usage_text`.

## Test

- Red first: a roster whose only cli seat fails must make `request` exit
  non-zero. Run it against the current script and watch it pass, which is the
  defect.
- A roster where one of two seats fails still exits 0 — per-seat fail-open is not
  what is changing, and a test that cannot tell the two apart would licence
  breaking it.
- A forge-bot-only roster exits 0.

## Invariants

- Per-seat fail-open (0205 rule 2) is untouched.
- `harvest`, `scorecard`, `scores` and `audition` keep their exit semantics.
- No seat's credential is read, logged or named by value anywhere.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `request` exits non-zero when every attempted cli/model seat failed
- [ ] The three cases above are tested, and the first was seen red
- [ ] `usage_text` names the two repository levers
- [ ] `make check` passes
